# Algorithm for file updates in Python

## Project description

For this scenario, I am working in security at a health care company where part of my job is to keep an allow list file updated with the IP addresses of employees who are allowed to access personal patient records. To do this, I wrote a Python algorithm that checks the allow list against a separate remove list of IP addresses that should no longer have access. If any IP address from the remove list is found in the allow list, the code removes it and updates the original file with the new list. Doing this with Python saves time and helps make sure people who should not have access are removed properly.

---

## Open the file that contains the allow list

```python
import_file = "allow_list.txt"

with open(import_file, "r") as file:
```

First, I needed to open the file that holds the allowed IP addresses. To do this, I stored the file name "allow_list.txt" in a variable called "import_file". Then, I used a "with" statement together with the "open()" function to open the file and make sure Python closes it automatically once it is done. Inside "open()", I passed in "import_file" as the file to open and "r" so it opens in read mode, and I used "as file" to store the opened file in the variable "file".

---

## Read the file contents

```python
with open(import_file, "r") as file:
    ip_addresses = file.read()
```

Next, I needed to read what was inside the file so I could work with the IP addresses. Inside the "with" statement, I used the ".read()" method on the "file" variable to turn the contents of "allow_list.txt" into a string. I then saved that string into a new variable called "ip_addresses".

---

## Convert the string into a list

```python
ip_addresses = ip_addresses.split()
```

Because "ip_addresses" was still one big string, I could not remove single IP addresses from it yet. To fix this, I used the ".split()" method on "ip_addresses" to break the string up by whitespace and turn each IP address into its own item inside a list. I then saved this new list back into the "ip_addresses" variable.

---

## Iterate through the remove list

```python
for element in remove_list:
```

After getting the allow list into a list format, I needed a way to go through every IP address in "remove_list". To do this, I set up a "for" loop using the header "for element in remove_list:". Here, the "for" and "in" keywords tell Python to step through "remove_list" one item at a time and temporarily store each IP address in the loop variable "element".

---

## Remove IP addresses that are on the remove list

```python
for element in remove_list:
    if element in ip_addresses:
        ip_addresses.remove(element)
```

Inside the "for" loop, I added an "if" statement ("if element in ip_addresses:") to check if the current IP address in "element" is also in the "ip_addresses" list. When it finds a match, it runs "ip_addresses.remove(element)", using the ".remove()" method to take that IP address out of "ip_addresses". Using the ".remove()" method like this works because there are no duplicate IP addresses in the "ip_addresses" list.

---

## Update the file with the revised list of IP addresses

```python
ip_addresses = "\n".join(ip_addresses)

with open(import_file, "w") as file:
    file.write(ip_addresses)
```

Once the unwanted IP addresses were removed, I needed to put the updated list back into "allow_list.txt". First, I used "\n".join(ip_addresses) so the ".join()" method would turn the list back into a string with each IP address on a new line. Next, I used another "with" statement and "open(import_file, "w") as file:", using "w" to open the file in write mode so it overwrites the old contents. Finally, I used "file.write(ip_addresses)" with the ".write()" method to save the updated IP addresses into the file.

---

## Summary

In this activity, I created a Python algorithm to automatically update an allow list file of approved IP addresses. First, I used a "with" statement, "open()", and ".read()" to load the contents of "allow_list.txt" as a string, and then used ".split()" to turn it into a list. Next, I used a "for" loop and an "if" condition to check each IP address in "remove_list" against "ip_addresses", using ".remove()" to delete any matches. Finally, I used "\n".join() to turn the updated list back into a string and used another "with" statement with "w" mode and ".write()" to overwrite the original file. Using a script like this makes updating access files much faster and helps avoid mistakes.
